{
  "abstract": "For an N-vertex graph G, let z = log N and F = max(1, rank over F₂ of its zero-diagonal adjacency matrix divided by z). There are absolute positive constants b,c such that F ≤ c(log z)^(1/3) implies reg(G) > z^(1+b/F) for all sufficiently large N, uniformly in G and F. The formalization proves the fixed-rank and critical-rank corollaries, a sparse off-diagonal error reduction with retained order N²/(2N+4e), and the corresponding strict uniform bound and its particular consequences for minimum binary rank over diagonal completions. The proof constructs physical trace cells, proves their mixing and candidate estimates, replaces controller packets with prescribed integer row sums, and constructs and installs a reserve correcting every bounded even-total degree defect.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Arthur Ramos"
    }
  ],
